{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Independent CloudWatch sweep corroborates the mechanism — plus one thing it does NOT explain\n\nA parallel agent's presence sweep (period=300, 96 buckets/metric, 8 h) landed while this was in\nreview. It is an independent instrument — CloudWatch presence, not the host journal — and it\nmeasures the same thing my breadcrumb trace does, from the other end:\n\n    lines 112-213  (head)         93% present   max gap 10 min\n    line  601  docker_storage     70%                   35 min\n    lines 622/640 buildkit        31%                   60 min\n    line  798  var_tmp_used        0%                    -\n    lines 1193-1301 counters   48-53%                 60-70 min\n    lines 1463/1511 g2p/mirror    43%                   85 min\n\n**Monotone decline in presence with line number.** That is the positional mechanism measured\nfrom the metric side, and it agrees with the journal trace metric-for-metric: 93% at the head,\nfalling off sharply at the docker section (line 601 is the first metric after the walk), and\n`var_tmp_used_percent` at **0%** — a metric that has never once been reached.\n\nTwo consequences worth recording.\n\n**The consumer dependency is satisfied by this change.** A downstream agent has documented an\noperator workaround — \"a reading-alarm firing on absence means the probe could not measure\nthis\" — because with the probe dead before section 2, `disk_used_percent` is absent for reasons\nhaving nothing to do with the volume, and the alarm cannot tell \"the disk is full\" from \"the\nprobe never measured it\". That information is genuinely not in the metric today. `probe_ok`,\n`probe_elapsed_seconds` and `probe_truncated` are in change 2684 and turn that inference into a\nfact. The consumer's guidance can become a lookup instead of a heuristic once it lands.\n\n**NEGATIVE RESULT — the 25-minute HEAD gap is not explained by this root cause, and I am not\nclaiming it.** The same sweep reports the head-of-script max gap widening from 10 to 25 minutes,\nand four false alarms at 20:47Z on missing data while the conditions were verified healthy. The\nhead runs *before* the docker walk: my breadcrumb trace shows the prologue completing in 7-13\nseconds on every one of ten killed runs, and the gate-scratch breadcrumb landing every time. So\na 25-minute head gap is roughly four to five missed runs at the observed 5-6 minute cadence, and\n**my root cause cannot produce it.** Something else is dropping whole runs or failing the\n`aws put-metric-data` calls at the head. That is a second defect and deserves its own ticket\nrather than being folded in here on a plausible story — which is the mistake this whole\ninvestigation exists to correct.\n\nOne checkable prediction rather than a claim: each killed run currently *occupies* 240 s of a\n~300 s timer period, leaving ~60 s of slack, so any additional delay pushes a run into the next\nperiod. After this change a run takes ~105 s, restoring ~195 s of slack. **If the head gap\nnarrows after deploy, cadence pressure was a contributing factor; if it does not, the second\ndefect is independent and the follow-up ticket is required.** Either outcome is informative,\nand `probe_elapsed_seconds` is what will settle it — today the run duration is visible only in\n`systemctl`, never in a metric, which is precisely the gap this change closes.\n\nFiled as **change 2684** — https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/2684\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788642289708744573,"uuid":"8187ced5-b298-440e-8d76-1658a36b761f"}